WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.274
TLS readiness begins with endpoint discovery.

2
00:00:03.274 --> 00:00:06.220
Public websites are only the visible part.

3
00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:25.536
Organizations also run APIs, internal services, load balancers, ingress controllers, reverse proxies, API gateways, service meshes, mobile backends, partner integrations, administrative consoles, monitoring endpoints, developer portals, and cloud-managed services.

4
00:00:25.536 --> 00:00:33.884
Each endpoint may have different owners, different clients, different libraries, and different tolerance for change.

5
00:00:33.884 --> 00:00:56.146
A useful inventory records the certificate, hostname, service owner, data classification, exposure, client population, TLS library, runtime version, platform, proxy path, cloud provider, CDN provider, hardware security module use, key management system, renewal process, and dependency on third-party products.

6
00:00:56.146 --> 00:01:08.341
That sounds like a lot, but without this context a team cannot tell which systems are easy pilots and which systems require vendor coordination or long client-update timelines.

7
00:01:08.341 --> 00:01:11.451
Discovery should combine multiple sources.

8
00:01:11.451 --> 00:01:14.970
External scanning can identify public endpoints.

9
00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.963
Configuration management can reveal load balancers and ingress rules.

10
00:01:19.963 --> 00:01:24.382
Certificate management systems can expose issuance patterns.

11
00:01:24.382 --> 00:01:27.820
Cloud inventories can show managed TLS services.

12
00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:32.239
Service mesh configuration can reveal internal traffic paths.

13
00:01:32.239 --> 00:01:37.805
Application teams can explain clients and data flows that automated tools miss.

14
00:01:37.805 --> 00:01:41.815
The goal is a living inventory, not a one-time screenshot.

15
00:01:41.815 --> 00:01:48.526
Cryptographic inventory is the foundation for PQC readiness because it connects risk to action.

16
00:01:48.526 --> 00:01:54.910
If a high-value API depends on an old TLS library, the next step may be runtime modernization.

17
00:01:54.910 --> 00:02:02.522
If a mobile app pins certificates or restricts algorithms, the next step may be client compatibility testing.

18
00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:06.041
If a CDN terminates TLS, vendor readiness matters.

19
00:02:06.041 --> 00:02:12.671
Inventory turns abstract migration into a list of owners, dependencies, tests, and decisions.
